<div class="proof">
<p>From <a href="#eq:Fs-Ps-relation">(4)</a>, <a href="#prop:hitting-probs-F">()</a> and
Proposition <a href="#prop:mean-num-visits-P">()</a> we can deduce  and</p>

<p>In the case <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> is easy to prove . <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>
implies , using reductio ad absurdum in
</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>There is an equivalence between:</p>
<ol>
<li><math><mo>∃</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> (note we will name this  afterwards)</li>
<li></li>
<li><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></li>
