</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Since , clearly i. is equivalent to ii.</p>
<p>ii. is equivalent to iii. can be deduced from  and using similar
arguments to the ones in the previous proof.</p>
</div>
<h3 id="classification-of-states">Classification of states</h3>
<h4 id="communicating-classes">Communicating classes</h4>
<div class="definition">
<p>The state <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> communicates with <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math>, and we will write it like <math><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></math>
iff <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> for some <math><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. It can be proven it is a transitive relation
using Proposition <a href="#prop:chapman-kolmogorov">()</a>.</p>
<p>If  and  then we will say that both states communicate and
we will represent it as .</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>State <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> is essential if  always implies . We denote the set
of essential states by <math><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>If <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> and , then <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Given , then by transitivity . Since <math><mi>i</mi></math> is essential, ,
which applying transitivity again from , gives us .</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p> is an equivalence relation in <math><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>, and we can decompose:</p>
<math display="block"><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>
<p>where for every <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math>, it holds .</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>We have:</p>
<ol>
<li>Reflexivity holds because given <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>, and <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> for some <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> then <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> and  using previous Lemma <a href="#lemma:transitive-es">()</a>.</li>
<li>Symmetry holds trivially.</li>
<li>Transitivity holds because if <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> then <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></li>
</ol>
</div>
<p>For a DTMC, we can reorder the states of the matrix: let <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> be the transition matrix for the
states in <math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> (which only communicate with themselves); <math><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> the probabilities of
going from <math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> to <math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> (and never returning); and <math><mi>R</mi></math> the probability
of the non-essential states communicating with themselves. The transition (stochastic by rows)
matrix we get is:</p>

<div class="definition">
<p>If we consider an ordered finite set <math><mi>S</mi></math> and it holds:</p>
<ol>
<li>States are ordered inside each class, <math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> included.</li>
<li><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> where <math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> is the minimum of <math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> and <math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> for all <math><mi>i</mi></math>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then we can say <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a> is the canonic form for the Markov chain.</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>A Markov chain where there only exists a communicating class is called irreducible (or ergodic)
Markov chain.
</p>
</div>
<h4 id="recurrence-and-transience">Recurrence and transience</h4>
<div class="definition">
<p>An state <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> is called recurrent iff <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and transient iff <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>
</div>
<p>Hence <math><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> diverges when <math><mi>i</mi></math> is recurrent and converges when <math><mi>i</mi></math> is transient.</p>
<div class="proposition">
<p>If <math><mi>i</mi></math> is a recurrent state and <math><mi>i</mi></math> communicates with <math><mi>j</mi></math>, then <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>,
and <math><mi>j</mi></math> is recurrent. Therefore, all the states of a communicating class are either
recurrent or transient.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>By Proposition <a href="#prop:fij-equivs">()</a>, <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Since <math><mi>i</mi></math> is recurrent <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>
and by Equation <a href="#eq:fij-recurrence">()</a>:</p>
<math display="block"><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math>
<p> means there exists <math><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math> verifying:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.1667em" rspace="0.1667em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>
<p>Since <math><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, and <math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, then it must hold <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> for all
<math><mi>k</mi></math> such that <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. In particular <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, and we deduce:</p>
<math display="block"><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math>
<p>We get <math><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math> by an analogous argument, and recursively we arrive at <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>
<p>Since <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> then by Proposition <a href="#prop:fij-equivs">()</a>
we deduce there exist <math><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></math>: <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.
So for every <math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></math> we arrive at:</p>
<math display="block"><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>
<p>Since <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, then <math><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></math> by Proposition <a href="#prop:fii-equiv">()</a>,
and therefore <math><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></math></p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>Again from Proposition <a href="#prop:fii-equiv">()</a> we deduce <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. We have proved <math><mi>j</mi></math> is
recurrent, but also that , and we can deduce <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> with an identical procedure.</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We will say a communicating class is recurrent (resp. transient) iff all of its states
are recurrent (resp. transient). We will say a communicating class <math><mi>C</mi></math> is closed if it
holds  and <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></math> implies <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>All the closed irreducible subchains of a DTMC are recurrent, and every DTMC has
at least one subchain of such type. A state is transient iff it is non-essential.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Let us fix some <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math>. We have</p>

<p>We conclude there exits at least one , so the subchain which
contains <math><mi>j</mi></math> is recurrent. The same argument can be applied to the other subchains.</p>
</div>
<div class="corollary">
<p>In a DTMC, a communicating class is recurrent iff it is closed.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>If we have a non closed class <math><mi>C</mi></math>, there exist <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></math> such that  and .
Therefore <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> by Proposition <a href="#prop:fij-equivs">()</a>.</p>
<p>Therefore:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><munder><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></math>
<p>and the class would not be recurrent.</p>
<p>Reciprocally, Proposition <a href="#prop:one-recurrent-class">()</a> gives us the result we want.</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We define the mean recurrence time for <math><mi>i</mi></math> as
</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We say that a recurrent state <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> is:</p>
<ol>
<li>Positive recurrent iff <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></math>.</li>
<li>Null recurrent iff <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></math>.</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>A state <math><mi>i</mi></math> is recurrent positive iff:</p>

<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Let us observe that for the limit to exist it must be <math><mrow><msub><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>
<math display="block"><mrow><munder><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo rspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><munder><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mi>n</mi><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>
<p>The second part is deduced from Equation <a href="#eq:Fs-Ps-relation">(4)</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>If <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></math> are communicating recurrent states, then they are both positive recurrent or null recurrent.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>By the procedure used in the proof of Proposition <a href="#prop:recurrent-classes">()</a>, we have:</p>
<math display="block"><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace width="1em"/><mo>∀</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></math>
<p>Therefore:</p>
<p></p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo rspace="0">=</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo rspace="0">≥</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo rspace="0">≥</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo rspace="0">=</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>Hence:</p>
<math display="block"><mrow><munder><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace width="0.1667em"/><mrow><munder><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo></math>
<p>And exchanging the roles of <math><mi>i</mi></math> and <math><mi>j</mi></math>, we deduce <math><mi>i</mi></math> is positive recurrent iff <math><mi>j</mi></math> is.</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>Every irreducible DTMC is positive recurrent</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Let us fix a <math><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></math>. We have, using that <math><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>:</p>

<p>Taking <math><msub><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> we have <math><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><munder><mo stretchy="false" lspace="0" rspace="0">→</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>
<p>Therefore, there must exist <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> with <math><mrow><msub><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">↗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. By Lemma <a href="#lemma:positive-rec-characterization">()</a>,
we deduce <math><mi>j</mi></math> is positive recurrent, therefore recurrent. By Proposition <a href="#prop:recurrent-classes">()</a> all states
communicating with <math><mi>j</mi></math> are also recurrent, and by Proposition <a href="#prop:all-positive-all-null">()</a>, all the states of
the chain are positive recurrent.</p>
</div>
<h4 id="absorption">Absorption</h4>
<p>Although the concept of absorbing state has arisen before, we give a formal definition.</p>
<div class="definition">
<p>A state <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math> is absorbing iff <math><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>
</div>
<p>Let us observe the matrix <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a>. This matrix poses some interesting questions for
the non-essential states. Let us take a non-essential state, namely <math><mi>i</mi></math>. Then it is likely the chain
will go to a state in <math><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> at some point, and never come back.</p>
<div class="definition">
<p>We define the absorbing time as <math><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>
If <math><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></math>, we can define the entry absorbing state as the value <math><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></math>.</p>
<p>We define:</p>
<ol>
<li>The absorbing probability as .</li>
<li>Taking <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>, we can define the probability of being absorbed by <math><mi>j</mi></math> as </li>
<li>Since we can decompose <math><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math> by the Proposition <a href="#prop:equiv-classes">()</a>. Given <math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>, we can define the probability of the state <math><mi>i</mi></math> being absorbed by the subchain <math><mi>C</mi></math> as:</li>
</ol>

</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>The following holds for each <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>:</p>
<ol>
<li><math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∉</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></math></li>
<li><math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∉</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></math></li>
<li><math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∉</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></math></li>
</ol>
</div>
<p>It is obvious that <math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></math>. Once the chain enters the
subchain <math><mi>C</mi></math>, it does so with initial probabilities
<math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">/</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></math>.</p>
<p>It we call , we have, taking a look at <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a>
that <math><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></math>.</p>
<p>We will note  to the column vector <math><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
or the row vector <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>.</p>
<p>Also, we can define <math><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> the matrix of transition probabilities from
<math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> to <math><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>, and  (the row-wise sum of $Q$). And we will call
<math><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></math>,
<math><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></math>.</p>
<div class="proposition">
<p>The following relations hold:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>Q</mi></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%;text-align: right;justify-items: end;" id="prop:Fast-matrix"><mtext>(5)</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%;text-align: right;justify-items: end;" id="prop:fast-matrix"><mtext>(6)</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>The distribution for <math><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> is 
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Let us fix <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math>. We have:</p>

<p>Also, using <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math>, we have:</p>

<p>Let us note that this last equality is <a href="#prop:Fast-matrix">(5)</a> expressed matrix-wise.
To get <a href="#prop:fast-matrix">(6)</a>, it suffices to sum in <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> in
Equation <a href="#eq:tau-ast-inf">()</a>. And summing in <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> in <a href="#eq:tau-ast-nat">()</a> we arrive at:</p>

</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>The matrix <math><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0">:</mo><mo lspace="0">=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is called fundamental matrix for the Markov chain. The entry <math><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math> represents the
number of times the process is in transient state <math><mi>j</mi></math> if it is started in also transient state <math><mi>i</mi></math>
(counting the initial state as <math><mn>1</mn></math> step).</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We will call <math><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> to the mean number of steps before the chain is absorbed, given that the chain starts in <math><mi>i</mi></math>,
with <math><mi>i</mi></math> a transient state.</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>If <math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> is finite, then <math><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> exists, <math><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi></math> and:</p>

<p>where the last equation tells us the absorption would take place almost surely.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>It holds:
<math display="block"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></math>
and therefore <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> (finiteness is used in this affirmation).</p>
<p>It is clear that if we take <math><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, then <math><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math> represents the mean number of
steps the chain hits <math><mi>j</mi></math> if it starts at <math><mi>i</mi></math> (counting the initial state). Thus, .</p>
<p>Since the rows of the transition matrix from <math><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></math> sum one,</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>
<math display="block"><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></math>
</div>
<h3 id="ergodicity">Ergodicity</h3>
<p>Let us remember the definition of irreducibility, which has arisen before:</p>
<div class="definition">
<p>We say that a Markov chain is ergodic (or irreducible) iff all states communicate with each other</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We say that a Markov chain is regular iff there exists some power of its transition matrix with
all positive elements</p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>Every regular Markov chain is also ergodic. The opposite does not hold.</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>If the Markov chain is regular, then there exists  such that for every <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math>, then
<math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and therefore , with <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></math> arbitrary states.</p>
<p>For the counterexample, it suffices to take the Markov chain given by <math><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>If <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is an ergodic transition matrix for a Markov chain with states <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>, then
it is possible to reach each state <math><mi>j</mi></math> from <math><mi>i</mi></math> in <math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math> steps at most.</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>The proof is based on graphs. Given a path of positive transition probabilities from
<math><mi>i</mi></math> to <math><mi>j</mi></math>, <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math>, since <math><mi>S</mi></math> is finite, if there were two states repeated,
<math><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math> with <math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>l</mi></math>, we could delete the subpath <math><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and we would still
have a path of states that would take us to <math><mi>j</mi></math>. Therefore we cannot have repeated elements in that path,
and <math><mi>n</mi></math> can be at most <math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>Given <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> regular, then <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></math> is regular for every 
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular, so all of its entries are positive. Therefore <math><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mi>P</mi></math> gives a matrix of positive entries,
and we can do induction on the number of products.</p>
</div>
<p>Lemma <a href="#lemma:power-regular">()</a> makes possible to define:</p>
<div class="definition">
<p>Given <math><mi>P</mi></math> a regular matrix, we will call </p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>If <math><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> is a numerable set, we can define <math><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0">:</mo><mo lspace="0">=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where
</p>
<p>It can be proven this quantity does not depend on the order of the set.</p>
</div>
<div class="theorem">
<p>Let <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> be a regular matrix. Let <math><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> the set of lengths
of all directed paths of <math><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> which start and end at the node <math><mi>i</mi></math>, with <math><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></math>. Let
<math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>. Then <math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular iff <math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></math>.</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>We are going to prove only that if <math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular, then <math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></math>, since this part
will be used by another result later on. The counterpart is also true, although it lies out of the
scope of this work.</p>
<p>Since <math><mi>A</mi></math> is regular, there exists  such that <math><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Because of Lemma
<a href="#lemma:power-regular">()</a> we have <math><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> for all <math><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Therefore there exists a path of
(large enough) prime length in each <math><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> and <math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="theorem">
<p>If <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is regular and the shortest cycle in <math><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> has length <math><mi>s</mi></math>, then
<math><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>, and <math><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>We may assume, w.l.o.g. that we have a shortest cycle formed by the sequence with no repeated elements
given by <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math>.</p>
<p><math><mi>P</mi></math> is ergodic (regular <math><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></math> ergodic) and therefore there exists a path from every node <math><mi>i</mi></math> to every other node
<math><mi>j</mi></math>, and that path has at most length <math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math> by Lemma <a href="#lemma:reachability">()</a>.</p>
<p>Let us split <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and we partition:</p>
<math display="block"><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.1667em">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
<p>Since <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> forms a cycle we can go from every <math><mi>i</mi></math> to another <math><mi>j</mi></math> with <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>.
Therefore there must be at least one positive element in each row of <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></math>. From the fact that the matrix
is irreducible, in <math><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></math> steps we must be able to go from one state in <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> to another
state <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> and stay inside the path <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> for the remaining
<math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></math> steps. Therefore every row in <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></math> must contain one positive value.</p>
<p>Since <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> forms a cycle in <math><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> we have a self cycles <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> in <math><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>.
<math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular and by Lemma <a href="#lemma:power-regular">()</a> <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></math> is also regular and thus ergodic. Therefore,
there must exist a path <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> of length <math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math> at most, with <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>. And
looping enough steps in the cycle <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> before, we have that <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>
<p>To end the proof, it suffices to write:</p>
<math display="block"><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
<p>and using the fact that if we have a matrix <math><mi>A</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, then <math><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> (and analogously for
row-wise products), we have shown <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="corollary">
<p><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is regular iff <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>One implication is clear by the definition of regular.</p>
<p>The counterpart: if <math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular, then there are cycles. If the shortest one had length <math><mi>m</mi></math>
(all have length lower or equal than <math><mi>m</mi></math> because we need at most <math><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math> steps to go from one state to
anoher), then by Theorem <a href="#th:regular-characterization">()</a> <math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></math> for all <math><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></math>
and matrix could not be regular. Then the shortest cycle must have length <math><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, and by
Theorem <a href="#th:regular-gamma-bound">()</a> we would have:</p>
<math display="block"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>If <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is ergodic and has some <math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></math> diagonal entries strictly positive, then
<math><mi>P</mi></math> is regular and <math><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math>
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><math><mi>A</mi></math> must be regular, since there would exist at least one cycle <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math>, and the matrix is ergodic, so we
can have as many loops as necessary and then a path <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> (of length lower or equal than $m$) or
<math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> and appropriate number of loops in <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math>, or junction of paths <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> plus
enough loops in <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math>, plus the path <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math>. To sum up, we can prove <math><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>
<p>Now we know <math><mi>A</mi></math> must have <math><mi>d</mi></math> cycles with length one. Let us suppose, w.l.o.g., that they are at
<math><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></math> states. We can partition:</p>
<math display="block"><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.1667em">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;border-right: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
<p>and by a similar argument to the one in the demonstration of Theorem <a href="#th:regular-gamma-bound">()</a> we
can show that <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></math> and <math><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></math> contain at least one positive entry per row and that <math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and
<math><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. For this last part it suffices to use the argument that we can loop in <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> for <math><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>
times and then in <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math> steps we can go from <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> to
<math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>Given a matrix <math><mi>P</mi></math>, a row vector <math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></math> is called a fixed row vector
iff <math><mi>r</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math> (it is a left eigenvector for the matrix). Analogously, a column vector <math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
is called a fixed column vector iff <math><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math> (it is a right eigenvector for the matrix).</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p>We call steady state to a probability row vector <math><mi>v</mi></math> such that <math><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math></p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>Let <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> be a transition matrix with all its entries positve. Let us fix a probability column
vector <math><mi>y</mi></math> and name <math><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>,
<math><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></math>. Hence, there exists
<math><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></math> such that:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Let us call <math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo>&ApplyFunction;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. If <math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, it suffices to
take <math><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. If <math><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, then <math><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> (no entry is null). Since <math><mi>P</mi><mi>y</mi></math> can be
thought as a weighted average of the entries in <math><mi>y</mi></math>, the maximum value we can reach is bounded:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>
<p>and likewise for the smallest possible one:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>
<p>Hence:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>
<p>and we can take <math><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></math>. Note this minimum only depends on <math><mi>P</mi></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="theorem">
<p><strong><strong>Fundamental limit theorem for regular Markov chains</strong></strong></p>
<p>Let <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> be a regular transition matrix. Then <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></math> where
 is a probability vector and
<math><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>. Moreover, it holds:</p>
<ol>
<li><math><mi>w</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math> and every other fixed row vector <math><mi>r</mi></math> verifies <math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></math> for some <math><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></math></li>
<li> and every fixed column vector <math><mi>c</mi></math> holds  for some <math><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></math></li>
</ol>
<p>Therefore, a stochastic regular matrix has a unique steady state.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><em>First part</em></p>
<p>Firstly, let us prove the theorem for a matrix <math><mi>P</mi></math> with all its elements strictly positive. Given <math><mi>y</mi></math> a
probability column vector, let us call:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">max</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">min</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%;text-align: right;justify-items: end;" id="eq:mns-sandwich"><mtext>(7)</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>Since each time we multiply a vector by <math><mi>P</mi></math> we do a weighted average, its maximum element would be lower
than the previous maximum, and the contrary will happen for its minimum. Hence:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%;text-align: right;justify-items: end;" id="eq:ms-sandwich"><mtext>(8)</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>In other words, <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> and <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> are monotonic bounded sequences of numbers, and their limit exits:</p>
<math display="block"><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>m</mi><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></math>
<p>By Lemma <a href="#lemma:bound-pos-matrix">()</a></p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>
<p>with <math><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></math>. Therefore <math><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0">:</mo><mo lspace="0">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mspace/><mi>lim</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.1667em">&ApplyFunction;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <a href="#eq:mns-sandwich">(7)</a> let us observe that for
every probability vector <math><mi>y</mi></math> it holds
<math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mi>v</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>v</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>.</p>
<p>Let us observe that <math><mi>v</mi></math> depends on <math><mi>y</mi></math>. If we take the column probability vector with <math><mn>1</mn></math> only in the <math><mi>j</mi></math> -th
position, namely <math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math>, then calling <math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math> the limit that depends on <math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math>, we have that:
</p>
<p>In other words, <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>, and
<math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></math>.</p>
<p>Since <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and the sequence is strictly increasing, <math><mi>v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and therefore all the entries for <math><mi>w</mi></math> are
strictly positive.</p>
<p>To extend the demonstration to regular matrices <math><mi>P</mi></math>, it suffices to remember that by their definition there
must exist a <math><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> such that <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></math> has only positive entries. Hence, <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> and <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>
converge to the same limit. Equations <a href="#eq:mns-sandwich">(7)</a> and <a href="#eq:ms-sandwich">(8)</a> were obtained
for regular stochastic matrices, and therefore for sufficiently large <math><mi>n</mi></math> we can find a value <math><mi>k</mi></math> such that
<math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></math>. Then we can assure there is convergence of
<math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> and <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math> and use the rest of the demonstration for the case of positive entries.</p>
<p>Since <math><mi>y</mi><mi>P</mi></math> is a probability vector if <math><mi>y</mi></math> is a probability vector, <math><munderover><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and taking
limits in <math><mi>n</mi></math>, <math><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Finally, <math><mi>w</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math> implies that it must be a probability vector.</p>
<p><em>Second part</em></p>
<ol>
<li>If , then we deduce <math><mi>w</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math> row-wise. For other fixed row vector <math><mi>r</mi></math> it holds <math><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mo minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">)</mo><mi>w</mi></math></li>
<li>Since <math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>c</mi></math>, taking limits <math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>c</mi></math>, and therefore</li>
</ol>

</div>
<p>As consequence of this theorem, since each element <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> of <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></math> is <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> and each row tends
to the same value <math><mi>w</mi></math>, the probability of starting at state <math><mi>i</mi></math> and being in <math><mi>j</mi></math> after <math><mi>n</mi></math> steps is
independent of the starting state <math><mi>i</mi></math>. Theorem <a href="#th:steady-regular">()</a> has an analogous theorem for ergodic
Markov chains. We will need some previous lemma to show that.</p>
<div class="lemma">
<p>Given <math><mi>P</mi></math> an ergodic stochastic matrix, then <math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> is regular and they have the same
eigenvectors.
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> is stochastic: all the elements are non negative and the sum for the <math><mi>i</mi></math> -th row is:</p>
<math display="block"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>
<p>To show that it is ergodic, fixed <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></math>, we must show that there exists a power matrix
<math><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mi>A</mi></munder></math> such that
<math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Since <math><mi>P</mi></math> is ergodic, there exists <math><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></math> such that <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, and
<math><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></math> where all of the elements of <math><mi>R</mi></math> are non negative. Since its
diagonal is strictly positive Lemma <a href="#lemma:ergodic-pos-diagonal">()</a> ends our proof.</p>
<p>Given <math><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math>, then <math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math>. To prove the other implication:
let <math><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></math>, and therefore
<math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>v</mi></math>. Analogously for right eigenvectors.</p>
</div>
<div class="theorem">
<p><strong><strong>Fundamental theorem for ergodic Markov chains</strong></strong></p>
<p>If <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is an ergodic transition matrix, then there exists a unique steady state <math><mi>w</mi></math> with all its
elements strictly positive. Moreover, every left eigenvector <math><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math> is linearly dependent of <math><mi>w</mi></math>, and
every right eigenvector <math><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0.2778em">:</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math> is a multiple of .</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Lemma <a href="#lemma:ergodic-regular-rel">()</a> and Theorem <a href="#th:steady-regular">()</a> suffice to reach the result.</p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>An ergodic stochastic matrix <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> has a unique steady state <math><mi>w</mi></math> that can be computed as the
only solution of the system:</p>
<p>
</p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>By means of Theorem <a href="#th:steady-ergodic">()</a> we have unicity and that the system</p>
<math display="block"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;text-align: right;justify-items: end;"></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;"><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mtd><mtd style="border-top: 0.05em solid currentcolor;text-align: left;justify-items: start;"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;"></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>
<p>must have a unique solution.</p>
</div>
<h3 id="steady-states">Steady states</h3>
<p>We are now in appropriate conditions for depicting how steady states of a general stochastic matrix can be computed.</p>
<div class="proposition">
<p>Given <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix, then every steady state <math><mi>v</mi></math> assigns <math><mn>0</mn></math> to the transient states, i.e. if
<math><mi>i</mi></math> -th state is transient, then <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Looking at the canonic matrix from Equation <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a>, if <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> are transient
states (we can suppose w.l.o.g. they are ordered as in the canonic form), we would have:</p>
<math display="block"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>
<p><math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> is invertible by Proposition <a href="#prop:i-r-invertible">()</a>. Hence, <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>
</div>
<p>Each matrix <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math> from <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a> is irreducible. Therefore, by the fundamental Theorem <a href="#th:steady-ergodic">()</a>,
each <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> has a unique steady vector <math><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></math>. If <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> corresponds to the states <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> We are going
to call .</p>
<div class="proposition">
<p>Given a stochastic matrix <math><mi>P</mi></math> written in its canonic form, then its space of steady states is a convex hull given by:</p>

</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>Since ,
and 
then it is clear that every convex combination of  with
<math><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></math>, is a steady state for the matrix.</p>
<p>They are also linearly independent, trivially, since they are not null by the Theorem <a href="#th:steady-ergodic">()</a> and the positive regions
of each one do not overlap by construction.</p>
<p>Given a steady state , by the Proposition <a href="#prop:ss-transient-zero">()</a> we know  for
<math><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></math> (the transient states). And by the shape of the matrix,  for each <math><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>, where
. Using Theorem <a href="#th:steady-ergodic">()</a>,
then <math><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></math> must hold. We deduce </p>
</div>
<h3 id="mean-first-passage-time">Mean first passage time</h3>
<div class="definition">
<p>Given an ergodic Markov chain, we call the expected number of steps to reach a state <math><mi>j</mi></math> from initial state <math><mi>i</mi></math>
as mean first passage time from <math><mi>i</mi></math> to <math><mi>j</mi></math>. We note it <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math>. Formally:</p>

<p>Formally, the only condition we should ask to be able to define <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math> with <math><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></math> would be that
<math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Obviously by the definition <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>
</div>
<div class="proposition">
<p>It holds</p>

<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>It holds: </p>
<p>On the one hand: <math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>. Therefore, and having in mind that <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mi>n</mi><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mi>n</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mi>n</mi><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">{</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>∞</mi></munderover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize="2.047em" maxsize="2.047em">}</mo></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;padding-right: 0;"></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;padding-left: 0;"><munder><mo>=</mo><mtext><a href="#eq:fij-recurrence">(??)</a></mtext></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>Analogously for <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>.</p>
</div>
<p>Matricially, we can define <math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>, <math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>,
<math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋱</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></math></p>
<p>And for the recurrence in Proposition <a href="#prop:mfpt-rec">()</a> we have:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%;text-align: right;justify-items: end;" id="prop:mfpt-rec-mat"><mtext>(9)</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<div class="proposition">
<p>Given an ergodic Markov chain, then <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></math> where <math><mi>w</mi></math> is its unique steady state.</p>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>By Equation <a href="#prop:mfpt-rec-mat">(9)</a> we have <math><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>.</p>
<p>Therefore , and <math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></math></p>
</div>
<div class="lemma">
<p>Let <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> an ergodic matrix, <math><mi>w</mi></math> its steady state and <math><mi>W</mi></math> the matrix formed by <math><mi>w</mi></math> as rows.
Then <math><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi></math> is invertible and <math><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> verifies:</p>
<ol>
<li>, <math><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">⋅</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math></li>
<li><math><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi></math></li>
</ol>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p></p>
<p>Let <math><mi>x</mi></math> such that <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Since <math><mi>w</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math> then <math><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math> and similarly
. Therefore <math><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Hence,
this means <math><mi>P</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></math>. By Theorem <a href="#th:steady-ergodic">()</a> this implies
, but <math><mi>w</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and <math><mi>w</mi></math> has only positive elements. Therefore <math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, and we can use
<math><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> is invertible iff <math><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace width="0.2777778em"/><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>
<p> and multiplying by <math><mi>Z</mi></math> in both sides: .
Similarly, we can show <math><mi>w</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></math>.</p>
<p>Since . Then <math><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="theorem">
<p>Given <math><mi>P</mi></math> an ergodic matrix, <math><mi>w</mi></math> its steady state, <math><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> and <math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math>,
it holds:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></math>
<p></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p>From Equation <a href="#prop:mfpt-rec-mat">(9)</a> we have <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></math> and:</p>

<p>and from that equation an <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, we deduce:</p>

<p>and therefore, subtracting and using Proposition <a href="#prop:ss-and-recurrence-prob">()</a>:</p>
<math display="block"><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></math>
</div>
<h2 id="r-package">R package</h2>
<p>This chapter describes the work carried out in <a href="https://github.com/spedygiorgio/markovchain">markovchain</a> package putting into practice the theoretical
knowledge acquired throughout previous chapters. The library is designed to be used with <code>R</code> language and
provides algorithms to perform analysis of Markov chains. We have focused in the discrete time homogeneous
part.</p>
<p>The package is coded both in <code>R</code> and <code>C++</code>. Our main objective was to propose mathematical driven improvements
to the aforementioned library. Most of the algorithms in this section are an original work and have been
designed by the author of this report. We have tried to take into account some existent algorithms as
starting point to propose improvements, such as <a href=""></a>. As a general guide for the additions to the
package to be compatible with the requisites and the good practices for <code>R</code>, we have used <a href=""></a>.</p>
<p>Code has been integrated in the upstream version for the library and is available at <a href="https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/markovchain/">CRAN</a>, the official
repository for R packages. To browse the added code, one could go to the repository that hosts the
project <sup><a href="#user-content-fn-anonymous.5" id="user-content-fnref-anonymous.5" data-footnote-ref="" aria-describedby="footnote-label">5</a></sup>. Due to space constraints, this section only
describes the algorithms, and does not contain listings of code.</p>
<h3 id="communicating-classes-based-algorithms">Communicating classes based algorithms</h3>
<p>We can propose an alternative definition of communicating property, taking <math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math> instead of <math><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,
which has been the one used in the software from the start:</p>
<div class="definition">
<p>The state <math><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></math> communicates with <math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></math>, and we will write it like <math><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></math>
iff <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math> for some <math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, where <math><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.2778em" rspace="0">:</mo><mo lspace="0">=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math></p>
</div>
<p>Proposition <a href="#prop:equiv-classes">()</a> was not valid in <math><mi>S</mi></math> because of lack of reflexivity,
which has been added with this new definition. Therefore, we can reformulate it as:</p>
<div class="proposition">
<p> is an equivalence relationship in <math><mi>S</mi></math></p>
</div>
<p>This modification does not affect further results.</p>
<p><code>commClassesKernel</code> (<code>.commClassesKernelRcpp</code> in the code) constitutes a core algorithm in the package,
since computation of communicating classes, classification of states or steady states algorithm depend
on it. It calculates a matrix of communicating classes (where the entry <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> is <math><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></math> iff $i  j$)
and whether the class <math><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></math> is closed ($v(i) = true$). The original algorithm was based on 
and run in  in worst cases.</p>
<p>We have improved it turning it into a  algorithm. This has been done treating the
problem as the directed graph problem of finding its strongly connected components. We have used a
modification of Tarjan’s algorithm from <a href=""></a>.</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix

{$\{L_1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, L_r\} = <math><mtext><code>Tarjan</code></mtext></math>(P)$}
{$C := (false)<sub>m× m</sub>, <math><mspace width="1em"/></math> v := (false, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, false)<sub>1 × m</sub>$}
{$k ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, r\}$}

{$<math><mtext><code>isClosed</code></mtext></math> = ∃ j∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}, j ¬∈ L_k: P(i, j) > 0$}


{$v[i] := <math><mtext><code>isClosed</code></mtext></math>$}

{$C<sub>ij</sub> := true$}






{<math><mtext><code>commClassesKernel</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> an adjacency matrix

{<math><mtext><code>i := 0</code></mtext></math>}
{$<math><mtext><code>disc := </code></mtext></math> (-1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, -1)<sub>1 × m</sub>, <math><mspace width="1em"/></math> <math><mtext><code>low := </code></mtext></math>(-1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, -1)<sub>1 × m</sub>$}
{${exploring := stack\{\}}, <math><mspace width="1em"/></math> <math><mtext><code>onStack := </code></mtext></math> (false, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, false)<sub>1 × m</sub>$}
{$<math><mtext><code>components := </code></mtext></math> ∅$}
{$v ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}

{<math><mtext><code>strongConnect(v)</code></mtext></math>}


{$<math><mtext><code>components</code></mtext></math> = \{L_1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, L_r\}$}

{<math><mtext><code>Tarjan</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<p>

 data structures passed by <math><mtext><code>Tarjan</code></mtext></math> algorithm

{$<math><mtext><code>disc[v] := </code></mtext></math> i, <math><mspace width="1em"/></math> <math><mtext><code>low[v] := </code></mtext></math> i$}
{<math><mtext><code>i := i + 1</code></mtext></math>}
{<math><mtext><code>exploring.push(v)</code></mtext></math>}
{$<math><mtext><code>onStack[v] := </code></mtext></math> true$}

{$w ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}

{$<math><mtext><code>disc[w]</code></mtext></math> = -1$}
{<math><mtext><code>strongConnect(w)</code></mtext></math>}
{<math><mtext><code>low[v] = min(low[v], low[w])</code></mtext></math>}
{<math><mtext><code>onStack[w]</code></mtext></math>}
{<math><mtext><code>low[v] = min(low[v], disc[w])</code></mtext></math>}



{<math><mtext><code>low[v] = disc[v]</code></mtext></math>}


{$w := <math><mtext><code>exploring.pop()</code></mtext></math>$}
{$L = L ∪ \{w\}$}
{<math><mtext><code>onStack[w] := false</code></mtext></math>}

{$<math><mtext><code>components := components</code></mtext></math> ∪ \{L\}$}


{<math><mtext><code>strongConnect</code></mtext></math>}

</p>
