<h3 id="hitting-probabilities">Hitting probabilities</h3>
<p>We did not found anything documented in the literature with respect to the computation of these probabilities
algorithmically, so we had tried to come up with our own algorithm. The first thought would be to re-write
the system <a href="#eq:fij-recurrence">()</a> matrix-wise:</p>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋱</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋱</mo></mtd><mtd><mo rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<math display="block"><mtable displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0" style="width: 100%"><mtr><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd><mtd><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;"><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd style="text-align: right;justify-items: end;"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd style="text-align: left;justify-items: start;"><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1667em">(</mo><mtable displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">⋮</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd style="width: 50%"></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>
<p>we end up with the system:</p>

<p>Clearly, we can solve this system, provided that it has solution, by blocks <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math>, but
the matrix <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math> may not be invertible in some cases. Our hypothesis is that if we treated the
closed communicating classes differently (using the canonic form from Equation <a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a>),
then we could delete the linearity in the system.</p>
<p>In conclusion using Corollary <a href="#corollary:comm-recurrence">()</a> and Proposition
<a href="#prop:recurrent-classes">()</a>, if we have a closed communicating class <math><mi>C</mi></math>,
then <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> for all <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></math> and <math><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> for all <math><mi>k</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></math>.
It can be proven that matrices we get after doing that substitution are invertible.
Solving a system <math><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></math> can be done in . Therefore since we have to solve <math><mi>m</mi></math>
systems, and complexity of <code>commClassesKernel</code> is , this procedure runs in .</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix

{$[C,v] = <math><mtext><code>commClassesKernel(P)</code></mtext></math>$}

{$j ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}



{$i ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}

{$k ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}
{$A_j(i,k) = δ<sub>ik</sub>$}

{$L<sub>j</sub>[i] = C<sub>ij</sub>$}






{<math><mtext><code>hittingProbabilities</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<p>Related to hitting probabilities, we have added an algorithm to compute the mean number of visits to
a state when we start in a given one, based on Proposition <a href="#prop:fii-equiv">()</a>, which is ,
since it needs to call Algorithm <a href="#alg:hittingProbs">()</a>.</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix

{$(f<sub>ij</sub>) = <math><mtext><code>hittingProbs</code></mtext></math>(P)$}
{$j ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}
{$<math><mtext><code>isOne := </code></mtext></math> f<sub>jj</sub> = 1$}
{not <math><mtext><code>isOne</code></mtext></math>}
{$r = {1 - f<sub>jj</sub>}$}

{$i ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}
{$f<sub>ij</sub> = 0$}
{$ = 0$}

{$ = <math><mtext><code>if isOne </code></mtext></math> +∞ <math><mtext><code> else </code></mtext></math> r ⋅ f<sub>ij</sub>$}




{$()<sub>m × m</sub>$}

{<math><mtext><code>meanNumVisits</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<h3 id="mean-first-passage-time-and-recurrence-times">Mean first passage time and recurrence times</h3>
<p>Based on Theorem <a href="#th:mfpt-computation">()</a>, we ported and polished the following algorithm to C++ (a
version of it was already in the package, although it did not made checkings about the matrix being
regular). Due to backward compatibility with the previous version, the algorithm can be called with an
ergodic matrix and a set of states <math><mtext><code>destination</code></mtext></math> and it would compute the mean first passage time
from the states not in <math><mtext><code>destination</code></mtext></math> to any of the states there. This algorithm is ,
since it computes an inverse matrix.</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> an ergodic matrix
 <math><mi>C</mi></math> destination set, <math><mrow><mspace/><mi>∅︀</mi></mrow></math> by default.


{Compute <math><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext><code>steadyStates</code></mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> (there is only one)}
{Compute $Z = (I - P + W)<sup>-1</sup>$}
{Compute <math><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.2222em" rspace="0.2222em">∗</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></math> for all $i, j ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}
{$M = (m<sub>ij</sub>)$}

{$M = <math><mtext><code>meanAborptionTime</code></mtext></math>(P, C)$}



{<math><mtext><code>meanFirstPassageTime</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<p>Based on Proposition <a href="#prop:ss-and-recurrence-prob">()</a> and having into account the canonic form
<a href="#eq:canonic-form">()</a>, we can compute the mean recurrence times for the transient states of a
Markov chain. Thus, the following algorithm has been added to the package ( complexity):</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix

{$(u_1 | <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math> | u_n) = <math><mtext><code>steadyStates</code></mtext></math>(P^t)$}



{$u<sub>ij</sub> > 0$}
{$r_j := u<sub>ij</sub><sup>-1</sup>$}



{$\{(j, r_j): r_j > 0\}$}

{<math><mtext><code>meanRecurrenceTime</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<h3 id="reachability">Reachability</h3>
<p>Let us define  for <math><mi>P</mi></math> a stochastic matrix.
Former algorithm to compute the reachability matrix (entry <math><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></math> is <math><mn>1</mn></math> iff , <math><mn>0</mn></math> otherwise)
was  since it computed <math><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. Using Algorithm
<a href="#alg:matrixPow">()</a> we can achieve a better  algorithm.</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> a stochastic matrix


{$B = <math><mtext><code>matrixPow</code></mtext></math>(A, m - 1)$}


{<math><mtext><code>is.accessible</code></mtext></math> algorithm}

</p>
<p>To compute reachability from a state single <math><mi>i</mi></math> to a state <math><mi>j</mi></math>, former algorithm was 
also, since it computed the whole reachability matrix. We have changed it for a DFS exploration, which would
be  at most.</p>
<p>

 <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math> an adjacency matrix
 <math><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></math>

{${exploring := stack\{i\}}$}
{$<math><mtext><code>v := </code></mtext></math> (false, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, false)<sub>1 × m</sub>$}
{$<math><mtext><code>reachable := </code></mtext></math> false$}
{<math><mtext><code>exploring</code></mtext><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mspace/><mi>∅︀</mi></mrow></math> and not <math><mtext><code>reachable</code></mtext></math>}
{$k := <math><mtext><code>exploring.pop()</code></mtext></math>$}

{$<math><mtext><code>reachable := </code></mtext></math> k = j$}
{$l ∈ \{1, <math><mo lspace="0">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0.1667em">.</mo><mo lspace="0" rspace="0">.</mo></math>, m\}$}
